    {
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50409438",
      "title": "Ask HN: If LLM reverse engineering is deemed illegal, is that precedent for OSS?",
      "source": "hackernews",
      "sourceName": "Hacker News",
      "summary": "With the efforts to clamp down on LLM-assisted reverse engineering - I am wondering;<p>If LLM assisted reverse engineering is deemed illegal by the courts, is that precedent for a class action that asserts OSS licenses are violated by labs that trained LLMs on that code?<p>There are two modalities for this;<p>1) LLM being post-trained against a decompiled binary - then used to output working source code.<p>2) LLM being used to analyze decompiled binary and used to output working source code.<p>How can it be &quot;clean room&quot; when an LLM is trained on or reads OSS but not clean room when the same is done for decompiled software?",
